IB‑STD‑001 — Directory Standard
Ironbridge Bank & Trust  
Domain: ironbridge.local  
Owner: IT & Identity Specialist

1. Purpose and Scope
This standard defines how Ironbridge Bank & Trust structures and manages its Active Directory environment.
Its purpose is to ensure the bank can clearly identify who has access and why, addressing MRA #1.
It applies to all user accounts, service accounts, computers, and security groups in ironbridge.local.
The standard improves consistency, supports audits, and reduces identity‑related risk.

2. OU Design
Every Organizational Unit (OU) exists for a specific reason: policy, delegation, or clarity.

Top‑Level OUs
Users — policy. Contains all human user accounts and supports consistent password and lifecycle policies.

Groups — clarity. Keeps role groups separate from users and computers for easier management.

Computers — policy. Ensures workstation policies apply cleanly and consistently.

Service Accounts — clarity. Isolates non‑human accounts for monitoring and control.

Disabled Users — policy. Stores leaver accounts for audit retention and investigation.

User Sub‑OUs
These OUs exist for delegation and clarity, aligned with the bank’s organizational structure.

Executive — delegation for HR/IT to manage executive accounts separately.

Lending — clarity for grouping lending staff.

Wire Operations — delegation supporting separation of duties.

Wealth Management — clarity for high‑risk roles.

Operations — clarity for back‑office staff.

Compliance — delegation supporting regulatory oversight.

IT — policy for privileged users requiring stricter controls.

Retail Banking — clarity; branch OUs beneath allow delegation to branch managers.

3. Naming Conventions
Consistent naming ensures predictability, automation, and auditability.

User Accounts: first initial + last name

Example: jsmith

Collision Handling: first two letters of first name + last name

Examples: jdoe, jadoe

Service Accounts: svc‑<function>

Example: svc-corebanking

Role Groups: ROLE‑<function>

Example: ROLE-LendingOfficer

Workstations: WKS‑SITE‑NNN

Example: WKS-DAL-014

4. Collision Rule (Worked Example)
When two users share the same first initial and last name:

John Doe → jdoe

Jane Doe → jadoe

If a third collision occurs, a number is added:

James Doe → jadoe2

This keeps usernames predictable and avoids inconsistent variations.

5. Required Attributes
Each account must include the following attributes, which support identity verification, automation, and audit requirements:

First Name / Last Name — identifies the person.

EmployeeID — unique identifier used for automation and HR alignment.

Department — supports role assignment and reporting.

Title — validates access appropriateness.

Manager — supports approval workflows.

Status — active or terminated; drives lifecycle actions.

Email — communication and identity verification.

These attributes ensure the bank can prove who the user is and why they have access.

6. Access Model
Ironbridge uses a strict role‑based access model (RBAC).

Access is granted only through role groups.

Each account receives one role group.

No direct permissions are assigned to any user.

Role groups map to job functions, not individuals.

This ensures access is based on job requirements, not personal preference.

7. Role Catalog Summary
The role catalog lists all role groups and the access each provides.
It includes business roles (e.g., Lending Officer, Teller, Wire Operator) and technical roles (e.g., IT Support, System Administrator).

Ownership
Compliance — owns the catalog and approves changes.

IT — maintains technical implementation.

HR — provides job titles and employment status updates.

Changes require approval from Compliance and IT.

8. Account States
Accounts may be in one of the following states:

Enabled — active employees.

Disabled — leavers or long‑term leave.

Locked — security event or failed login threshold.

Expired — temporary accounts past their end date.

Leaver accounts are disabled, not deleted, to preserve audit history, support investigations, and prevent accidental reuse.
Disabled accounts are moved to the Disabled Users OU.

9. Privileged Access Rules
Privileged access is tightly controlled to reduce risk.

Only IT staff may hold privileged roles.

Privileged accounts must be separate from standard user accounts.

All privileged access requires documented approval.

All privileged activity is logged and monitored.

Exception Register
Any deviation from this standard must be recorded with:

Reason for exception

Duration

Approver

Compensating controls

Compliance reviews exceptions monthly.

10. Review Cadence and Document Owner
Owner: IT & Identity Specialist

Review Frequency: Every 12 months or after major organizational changes

Approvals: Compliance and IT Management
